  "lesson_tasks": [
    {
      "id": "L44.formula-derivation",
      "prose_indices": [2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 11, 16, 17, 19, 21, 23, 27],
      "task_ids": ["L44.formula-derivation.proof"],
      "solution": [
        "For a != 0, divide ax^2+bx+c=0 by a; this is an equivalence, not a one-way implication.",
        "x^2+(b/a)x+c/a = (x+b/(2a))^2-(b^2-4ac)/(4a^2). Expand the square to check the identity.",
        "With D=b^2-4ac the equation is equivalent to (x+b/(2a))^2=D/(4a^2).",
        "If D>=0, factor the difference of squares as (x+b/(2a)+sqrt(D)/(2a))(x+b/(2a)-sqrt(D)/(2a))=0.",
        "The zero-product rule gives x=(-b-sqrt(D))/(2a) or x=(-b+sqrt(D))/(2a), and substituting either value into the factored equation proves sufficiency.",
        "For negative a, sqrt(D/(4a^2))=sqrt(D)/(2|a|). The signed denominator 2a in the plus/minus pair is still valid because changing its sign only exchanges the two roots; no identity sqrt(a^2)=a is assumed."
      ],
      "status": "complete"
    },
    {
      "id": "L44.real-root-cases",
      "prose_indices": [9, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 24, 25, 30, 31, 32, 33, 34],
      "task_ids": ["L44.real-root-cases.proof"],
      "solution": [
        "Since 4a^2>0, D<0 would require a nonnegative square to equal a negative number, so there is no real root.",
        "D=0 forces x+b/(2a)=0, so the sole root is -b/(2a). Both signs in the formula give this same root.",
        "D>0 gives two distinct real roots: their difference in absolute value is sqrt(D)/|a|>0.",
        "These conclusions assume a != 0; they do not classify linear degeneracies."
      ],
      "status": "complete"
    },
    {
      "id": "L44.example-1",
      "prose_indices": [36, 37, 39, 41, 43, 44],
      "task_ids": ["L44.example-1.solve"],
      "equation": "12x^2+7x+1=0",
      "coefficients": [12, 7, 1],
      "discriminant": 1,
      "formula": "x=(-7 +/- sqrt(1))/24",
      "real_roots": ["-1/3", "-1/4"],
      "verification": "12(x+1/3)(x+1/4)=12x^2+7x+1.",
      "status": "complete"
    },
    {
